sigsuspend passes the size of the mask, as rt_sigsuspend requires.

Linux's rt_sigsuspend takes the mask AND its size, and refuses any size
but sizeof(sigset_t) with EINVAL. picolibc passed the mask alone, so
the size was whatever the second argument register held, and every
sigsuspend returned -1 at once instead of sleeping. A caller that waits
in a loop -- waitpid with WNOHANG, then sigsuspend until SIGCHLD, as
gdbserver does -- spun at full speed instead of waiting, and on this
kernel that starved the very process it was waiting for.

--- a/libos/linux/sigsuspend.c
+++ b/libos/linux/sigsuspend.c
@@ -44,5 +44,5 @@
     for (sig = 1; sig < _NSIG; sig++)      /* 0 is no signal, in no set */
         if (sigismember(mask, sig))
             __kernel_sigset_set_mask(&kmask, _signal_to_linux(sig));
-    return syscall(LINUX_SYS_rt_sigsuspend, &kmask);
+    return syscall(LINUX_SYS_rt_sigsuspend, &kmask, sizeof(kmask));
 }
